{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/outcomes-at-opening/audit-c19-blind.md'","aggregated_output":"**The two new fields introduce several ways to lose or narrow a valid research question.** The biggest risk is treating “one figure” as one raw number, which can turn “next year compared with this year” into “next year’s number” and lose the comparison the analyst wanted to track.\n\nI ran the requested diffs. The system texts are identical, as are the closing checks. The schema diff adds the two required fields and their definitions; the only other difference is the final newline.\n\n### Sentence-by-sentence audit\n\n| New description | Verdict | Signed standard and assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| **`settles_on`, sentence 1:** “The one figure for one period … or the one event with its check date, whose result on that date settles this thesis.” | **Keeps narrower** | §3 says: “一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事” and gives “2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化” as an example. A change from one year to another is one tracked quantity, but “the one figure for one period” can be read as requiring one year’s raw figure. It also blurs the distinction in §3: “量是它的发布日；事件是能判出结果的那天.” A stated date for a figure is not necessarily its period or publication date. |\n| **`settles_on`, sentence 2:** “A thesis is a question, a fixed set of outcomes and a due date, written once: if no date and public material can place the answer in fixed outcomes … rewrite the thesis or do not propose it, and its statements are evidence.” | **Contradicts**, in part | The first clause matches the standard’s “一个问题句、一组结果档、一个到期日，写下后不改.” §4 says: “有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题.” But the unconditional “its statements are evidence” conflicts with §4’s “它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判.” Also, “several figures none of which leads” has **no source** as an automatic reason to rewrite or reject: §3 instead separates parts only when they can move apart **and** an analyst would bet on them separately. |\n| **`outcomes`, sentence 1:** “This thesis’s fixed outcomes, written now: five ranges of that one figure, or the states of that one event …; checkable, not overlapping and leaving no gap …” | **Keeps narrower** | The checks themselves match §3: “结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比.” The table supports five ranges for a quantity and two for an unsigned obligation. But “five ranges of that one figure” repeats the risk that a valid **change between two years** is reduced to one raw figure. For events, §3 permits “五档，或是非两档”; the description does not explicitly preserve the five-state option, although its wording does not prohibit it. |\n| **`outcomes`, sentence 2:** “A company thesis’s outcomes describe the business result; a shared quantity’s or policy’s describe only the result, not whether it is good.” | **Contradicts**, for policy | §3 says: “标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏.” A policy-driven question is not automatically a theme: the policy row says a question about “一家公司的收入、利润、份额或产品进度，算标的，即使由政策驱动.” The new sentence assigns **all** policy questions the theme treatment, including company questions. |\n\nD417 supports the particular refusal of a **non-annual rolling period with no publication day supplied by the topic or item**. It does not establish a general ban on every question mentioning multiple figures. Its recorded scope is the clean path’s later judging step, so applying its example here should not silently widen that decision.\n\n### Conflicts inside the request\n\n- **When to open:** “several figures none of which leads” can cause rejection or rewriting solely because several figures appear. The system instead asks whether the answer can change the business and whether the analyst would bet on it separately. It does not say a multi-figure question fails merely because no figure “leads.”\n- **Dates:** “whose result on that date” can suggest that the result itself must occur or be published on the check date. The system gives different rules by reason: next annual report for an undertaken obligation, a check within 18 months for an unsigned one, fiscal year-end for an undated pending regulatory decision, and a supported release or check date for shared quantities and product events. The new wording does not restate those distinctions and can pull the model toward a stricter reading.\n- **Evidence without a thesis:** “its statements are evidence” can be read as keeping every statement after a thesis is rejected. The system and closing check require each statement to pass the evidence test independently. A passing statement can link to an existing thesis or remain unlinked.\n- **Separate bets:** Rewriting a complex question to satisfy “one figure” or “one event” can create a smaller question that no longer merits a separate bet, even though the original question did. The system explicitly says to open only matters an analyst would bet on separately.\n\nThese are conflicts or ambiguities in the wording; this read-only review does not establish how often a model would act on them.\n\n### Industry neutrality\n\nThe new descriptions use generic concepts and name **no covered company, product or specific numerical business figure**. “Unsigned deal” and a rolling twelve-month survey are general examples. However, the policy sentence does **not** apply the same distinction across all cases: it treats policy questions as theme questions even when the standard classifies a policy-driven business outcome as a company question.\n\n### Concrete risks to valid questions\n\n- **Next year versus this year:** “one figure for one period” and “five ranges of that one figure” could change “How much will next year’s contract price rise or fall versus this year?” into “What will next year’s price be?” The first tracks a change; the second can lose it. The standard explicitly gives a next-year-versus-current-year comparison.\n- **Unsigned deal:** The example “whether an unsigned deal is signed” supports a yes/no question. But **“whose result on that date”** could be misread as requiring the signing to occur on the check date, instead of asking whether it was signed **by** that date. The standard allows an unsigned obligation to be checked within 18 months.\n- **Product shipment:** The same phrase could turn “Will shipments begin by the date?” into “Will shipments begin on that date?” or discourage a proposal when the article supports a check date but not the exact day shipments will begin. The standard tracks the main product reaching the revenue-producing step and checks it when the outcome can be determined.\n- **Several related figures:** “several figures none of which leads” could discard or narrow a single economically meaningful question before asking whether its parts can move apart and justify separate bets. Conversely, if they do justify separate bets, the standard calls for separate questions, each passing the opening tests.\n\nSources: [signed extraction standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:272), [D417](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4606), and [candidate schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c19-schema.json:51).","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"agent_message","text":"**Version 2 resolves the first audit’s main wording problems, but it still risks narrowing some valid event questions.** The phrase “whether it has happened by then” can turn a question about several meaningful outcomes into a yes/no question. For example, a regulatory process may be approved, rejected, or still pending at the check date; those are not necessarily the same as simply “happened” or “did not happen.”\n\nThe requested diffs confirm that c19’s system text and closing check are identical to c8’s. The schema adds the two required fields and their definitions; the only other difference is the final newline.\n\n### Sentence-by-sentence review\n\n| New description | Verdict | Signed standard and assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| **`settles_on`, sentence 1:** “one quantity for one period, which may be its change against a stated earlier period … or one event checked by a date (whether it has happened by then).” | **Keeps narrower**, for events | §3 says “一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事” and gives “2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化” as an example. The new text now preserves that comparison. But the same table allows an event “五档，或是非两档”; “whether it has happened” suggests a yes/no test even where the analyst tracks several states. |\n| **`settles_on`, sentence 2:** “If no date and public material can place the answer in fixed outcomes, rewrite the thesis; if it cannot be rewritten, do not propose it (whether its statements are kept is judged as evidence, section 4).” | **Consistent**, with a scope caution | §4 asks “有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题” and says “重写不了就不开，材料作证据.” The new parenthesis correctly makes keeping a statement a separate evidence judgment; §4 says “它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判.” The rewrite must still preserve what the source supports, rather than invent a date or change the matter being tracked. |\n| **`outcomes`, sentence 1:** “five ranges of that quantity or of its change, or the event’s states, five or two … checkable, not overlapping and leaving no gap …” | **Consistent** | §3 specifies quantities as “量，五档,” unsigned obligations as “还没签：是非两档,” and events as five or two. It also says “结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比.” The unsigned-deal example correctly asks whether signing occurred **by** the check date. |\n| **`outcomes`, sentence 2:** “A company thesis’s outcomes describe the business result, even when a policy drives it; a theme’s … describe only the result, not whether it is good.” | **Consistent** | §3 says “标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏.” Its policy row says a question about “一家公司的收入、利润、份额或产品进度，算标的，即使由政策驱动.” The revised sentence preserves that distinction. |\n\nOne schema detail remains: the array permits **2, 3, 4, or 5** items, while the description and standard specify **five or two**. The description tells the model the right rule, but the schema alone would also accept three or four outcomes.\n\n### First audit: what changed\n\nThe year-on-year issue is resolved in the wording: both fields explicitly allow ranges of the **change** against an earlier period. The prior “one figure” language no longer directs the model to replace the comparison with a raw annual value.\n\nThe evidence issue is resolved: rejected questions’ statements are judged as evidence, rather than automatically kept. The unsupported “several figures none of which leads” rejection is gone. The policy classification issue is resolved: a policy-driven company outcome is no longer automatically treated as a theme.\n\nThe earlier date concern is substantially improved: the unsigned-deal example says **“by the check date,”** and the event wording says **“by then,”** rather than implying the event must occur on the exact day. The remaining event-state concern above is new to this wording.\n\n### Conflicts with the rest of the request\n\nI found **no direct contradiction** with the system or closing check on the four named points:\n\n- **Opening:** the new fields add the standard’s checkability requirement; they do not say a checkable question opens without also being important and a separate bet.\n- **Dates:** they do not override the system’s different date rules for obligations, regulatory decisions, shared quantities and products. They also do not repeat those rules, so the model must still apply the system text.\n- **Evidence without a thesis:** the new wording sends statements to the evidence judgment. The system and closing check then link passing evidence to relevant existing questions or leave it unlinked.\n- **Separate bets:** neither description explicitly removes that requirement. There is still a risk that “rewrite the thesis” leads to a smaller question that no longer represents the original separate bet; the descriptions do not explicitly tell the model to preserve the tracked matter when rewriting.\n\n[D417](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4606) declines a non-annual rolling-period figure when neither the topic nor the item gives a publication day. Its recorded scope is the clean path’s ladder step. It does **not** support a general refusal of annual comparisons, undated unsigned obligations, or product events with a supported check date.\n\n### Industry neutrality and concrete risks\n\nThe new descriptions are industry-neutral and name no covered company, product, or specific business number. “Unsigned deal” and the calendar-year comparison are general examples.\n\n- **Next year versus this year:** explicitly supported by “its change against a stated earlier period” and “ranges … of its change.” I do not see the former raw-number narrowing in version 2.\n- **Unsigned deal:** explicitly supported as a yes/no question by the check date. The system still supplies the separate rule that the check date is within 18 months.\n- **Shipment by a date:** “whether it has happened by then” fits a shipment yes/no question and does not require shipment on the exact date. The system still requires a supported date.\n- **A multi-state event:** “whether it has happened by then” can pull a broader question toward yes/no despite the next field allowing five event states. That can lose the difference between outcomes an analyst tracks.\n- **Any rewritten question:** “rewrite the thesis” could produce a narrower, easier-to-check question that drops the original subject, scope, or economically meaningful comparison. This is a risk in interpretation, not proof that the model will do so.\n\nThese are wording findings from a read-only comparison; they do not establish how often the model would make these mistakes. Sources: [candidate schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c19-schema.json:51), [signed standard §§3–4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:272), and [first blind audit](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/outcomes-at-opening/audit-c19-blind.md)."}}
